{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FZxeJ8EHYTaxNUECwYbBcZ","type":"tool_result","content":"\\citet{LY2002} collected twelve such problems, from operations research, VLSI\ndesign and graph theory, in the table that Table~\\ref{tab:ly2002} reproduces.\nEach computes, on corresponding inputs, ``a function $f(\\Pi)$ that is either\nequal to the number of open stacks or closely related to it (plus or minus\none)'' \\citep[p.~1764]{LY2002}. The table gives a reference for each problem,\nbut no proofs and no graph of which problem reduces to which. ``Plus or minus\none'' has two readings. Under the \\emph{literal} reading, $f$ is within one of\nthe number of open stacks on every input. Under the \\emph{fixed-offset}\nreading, $f$ equals the number of open stacks plus one constant, the same on\nevery input. Only the second makes solving one problem the same as solving\nanother. We call the twelve \\emph{the pathwidth complex}, because pathwidth is\nboth the name the literature uses most (Section~\\ref{sec:names}) and the\nquantity through which every true relation in the table is proved\n(Section~\\ref{sec:equiv}). This paper asks: \\emph{which of the equalities in\nthe Linhares--Yanasse table are true, and what follows when results,\nalgorithms and benchmarks are moved between the twelve problems through\npathwidth?}\n\nThe table has a history that explains why the question is still open. It was\nfirst printed as Table~2.1 of the first author's doctoral thesis\n\\citep[p.~13]{Linhares2001}, supervised by the second author, which proved MOSP\nNP-hard and equal to gate matrix layout. Its rows were collected over the years\nof the doctorate, while reading the literature of each field. Each came with a\nreference and nothing else. No row's relation to open stacks was checked, no\npublished proof was restated, and no graph said which problem reduces to which.\nThe table appeared again, row for row, as Table~1 of \\citet{LY2002}, and it has\nbeen read since as a statement of equivalence.\n\\citet{GarciaDeLaBandaStuckey2007}, for instance, credit the speed of their\nsearch in this journal to ``the fact that the problem is fixed parameter\ntractable (Linhares and Yanasse 2002)''. So the table is already used to\njustify algorithms, and whether it is true matters. If it is true, every result\nabout any one of the twelve problems transfers to all the others. Looking back,\nthe table was the thesis's most important finding, and this article does what\nthe thesis did not: it states each problem precisely, proves what is true and\nrefutes what is not. The communities that could have checked the table\nrarely read each other: of 844 works that cite the table's papers,\nonly six cite both an open-stacks paper and a graph-theory paper\n(Section~\\ref{sec:names}).\n\n\\paragraph{What we find.}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\emph{The table, settled.} Eight of the twelve rows are exact, the\n  pathwidth row itself counted as one of them: the other seven equal pathwidth\n  or pathwidth plus one on every input, outside degenerate cases such as an\n  empty graph. Two rows, split bandwidth and edge\n  search, lie in bands one and two wide. Two rows, PLA folding and edge\n  separation, are false as stated, with counterexamples. A variant of each\n  false row is exact (Section~\\ref{sec:equiv}).\n\\item \\emph{One name.} The literature has settled on ``pathwidth'', which has\n  more than three times as many relevant works as the other eleven names\n  together. Besides pathwidth, the most active community over 2005--2024 is the\n  open-stacks one, and the exact search of Section~\\ref{sec:search} came\n  from it\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:names}).\n\\item \\emph{A repaired search.} The best exact algorithm for MOSP, the search of\n  \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, is also a pathwidth algorithm. Two of its published\n  dominance theorems are false, as are their later restatements\n  \\citep{Chu2011,Fink2012}. The correct rule was already known in the exact\n  pathwidth literature, and we show that it can be tested by one bipartite\n  matching per candidate. The repaired search is proved sound. Over each\n  benchmark set it changes the total number of search nodes by at most 2\\%, and no wrong answer of the\n  published rules on a whole instance is known (Section~\\ref{sec:search}).\n\\item \\emph{Benchmarks re-certified, and a dataset.} The repaired search\n  re-proved all 115 benchmark optima that rested on the published rules, and\n  none changed (as of 6 October 2026). We release 17,714 graphs, distinct up to\n  isomorphism, from twenty benchmark collections of the twelve problems; 16,087\n  have a certified pathwidth with a layout that attains it, and so a certified\n  value for every exact problem in the table (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\paragraph{How to read the paper.} Section~\\ref{sec:equiv} states the twelve\nproblems and proves what is true. Section~\\ref{sec:names} measures which names\nand communities the literature uses. Section~\\ref{sec:search} reads the exact\nopen-stacks search as a pathwidth algorithm, finds the two false rules and\nproves the repaired search sound. Section~\\ref{sec:results} reports its cost\nand the dataset, and Section~\\ref{sec:closing} answers the question above.\nEvery theorem we state is machine-checked in the Lean~4 proof assistant. The\nreader need not read Lean: the proofs are given in the text, and\nAppendix~\\ref{app:lean} maps each result to its formal statement.\nAppendix~\\ref{app:machinery-all} holds the supporting material, and the data,\ncode and proofs are described at the end of the paper (page~\\pageref{sec:data}).\n\\fbox{\\textbf{URL to be added on release}}. The repository holds:\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item the Lean~4 development, with every theorem the paper names\n  (Appendix~\\ref{app:lean}) and an axiom check that covers all of them;\n\\item the source of both solvers, the open-stacks customer search and the graph\n  pathwidth solver, in C and in Python, with the published and the repaired\n  rules;\n\\item the dataset of Section~\\ref{sec:dataset}, the certificates of\n  Section~\\ref{sec:practice} and the independent checkers of both;\n\\item the bibliometric data of Section~\\ref{sec:names}: the cached OpenAlex\n  responses, the relevance labels and the mechanical rule;\n\\item a script for every table, figure and number in the paper, each of which\n  regenerates it from the recorded data.\n\\end{itemize}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"89de9f17-006d-4b43-8d2c-1535745c26af","uuid":"ab29c149-6937-4490-8c1a-e466bd7b04e9","timestamp":"2026-10-06T16:07:35.422Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"\\citet{LY2002} collected twelve such problems, from operations research, VLSI\ndesign and graph theory, in the table that Table~\\ref{tab:ly2002} reproduces.\nEach computes, on corresponding inputs, ``a function $f(\\Pi)$ that is either\nequal to the number of open stacks or closely related to it (plus or minus\none)'' \\citep[p.~1764]{LY2002}. The table gives a reference for each problem,\nbut no proofs and no graph of which problem reduces to which. ``Plus or minus\none'' has two readings. Under the \\emph{literal} reading, $f$ is within one of\nthe number of open stacks on every input. Under the \\emph{fixed-offset}\nreading, $f$ equals the number of open stacks plus one constant, the same on\nevery input. Only the second makes solving one problem the same as solving\nanother. We call the twelve \\emph{the pathwidth complex}, because pathwidth is\nboth the name the literature uses most (Section~\\ref{sec:names}) and the\nquantity through which every true relation in the table is proved\n(Section~\\ref{sec:equiv}). This paper asks: \\emph{which of the equalities in\nthe Linhares--Yanasse table are true, and what follows when results,\nalgorithms and benchmarks are moved between the twelve problems through\npathwidth?}\n\nThe table has a history that explains