ed on ``pathwidth'', which has\n  more than three times as many relevant works as the other eleven names\n  together. Besides pathwidth, the most active community over 2005--2024 is the\n  open-stacks one, and the exact search of Section~\\ref{sec:search} came\n  from it\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:names}).\n\\item \\emph{A repaired search.} The best exact algorithm for MOSP, the search of\n  \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, is also a pathwidth algorithm. Two of its published\n  dominance theorems are false, as are their later restatements\n  \\citep{Chu2011,Fink2012}. The correct rule was already known in the exact\n  pathwidth literature, and we show that it can be tested by one bipartite\n  matching per candidate. The repaired search is proved sound. Over each\n  benchmark set it changes the total number of search nodes by at most 2\\%, and no wrong answer of the\n  published rules on a whole instance is known (Section~\\ref{sec:search}).\n\\item \\emph{Benchmarks re-certified, and a dataset.} The repaired search\n  re-proved all 115 benchmark optima that rested on the published rules, and\n  none changed (as of 6 October 2026). We release 17,714 graphs, distinct up to\n  isomorphism, from twenty benchmark collections of the twelve problems; 16,087\n  have a certified pathwidth with a layout that attains it, and so a certified\n  value for every exact problem in the table (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\paragraph{How to read the paper.} Section~\\ref{sec:equiv} states the twelve\nproblems and proves what is true. Section~\\ref{sec:names} measures which names\nand communities the literature uses. Section~\\ref{sec:search} reads the exact\nopen-stacks search as a pathwidth algorithm, finds the two false rules and\nproves the repaired search sound. Section~\\ref{sec:results} reports its cost\nand the dataset, and Section~\\ref{sec:closing} answers the question above.\nEvery theorem we state is machine-checked in the Lean~4 proof assistant. The\nreader need not read Lean: the proofs are given in the text, and\nAppendix~\\ref{app:lean} maps each result to its formal statement.\nAppendix~\\ref{app:machinery-all} holds the supporting material, and the data,\ncode and proofs are described at the end of the paper (page~\\pageref{sec:data}).\n\\fbox{\\textbf{URL to be added on release}}. The repository holds:\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item the Lean~4 development, with every theorem the paper names\n  (Appendix~\\ref{app:lean}) and an axiom check that covers all of them;\n\\item the source of both solvers, the open-stacks customer search and the graph\n  pathwidth solver, in C and in Python, with the published and the repaired\n  rules;\n\\item the dataset of Section~\\ref{sec:dataset}, the certificates of\n  Section~\\ref{sec:practice} and the independent checkers of both;\n\\item the bibliometric data of Section~\\ref{sec:names}: the cached OpenAlex\n  responses, the relevance labels and the mechanical rule;\n\\item a script for every table, figure and number in the paper, each of which\n  regenerates it from the recorded data.\n\\end{itemize}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
