{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01P7gkDmWR7KPFeKSjSkwXAD","type":"tool_result","content":"== 8\nThe second theorem is the one the rest of the paper uses. In Figure 1.1(d), the sets of customers active\nat each step of the good order are the bags of a path decomposition of the MOSP graph. The theorem says\nthat this always works, in both directions.\nTheorem 2.3 (MOSP is pathwidth plus one). If M has at least one 1, then Z(M) = pw(GM ) + 1 (Appendix A,\n== 9\n7. Lengauer’s vertex separator game: VSG(G) = max(1, vs(G)) (Lengauer, 1981, Thm 4) (Appendix A,\nA.11).\nFor the table, this makes the gate matrix layout, one-dimensional logic, interval thickness, node search\nand narrowness rows exact, and gives an exact variant of each false row. A single certified pathwidth\n== 10\n1,213\n\ngraph path-width\n110\n== 11\nTable 3.1: Besides pathwidth, the most active name over 2005–2024 is MOSP, although vertex separation led in 2015–\n2019. Relevant works per five-year period, for the three names with the most works over 2005–2024; the next are edge\nsearch (28) and node search (20).\nName\n== 12\nproves all 50 VSPLIB trees, against their 30, and 39 of the 73 Harwell–Boeing graphs, against their 26.\nThese are comparisons with published numbers obtained on other hardware, not a head-to-head run.\nTwo consequences shape the rest of the paper. First, a result on any of the twelve problems should be\nstated, and searched for, as a result on pathwidth. Section 4 does this for the customer search. It finds that\n== 13\nTheorem 4.2 (the search decides pathwidth). For nonempty C, Solk ( /0) ⇐⇒ pw(G) + 1 ≤ k; on the MOSP\ngraph of an instance M with a requirement, Solk ( /0) ⇐⇒ Z(M) ≤ k (Appendix A, A.17).\nProof. For a closing order c1 , . . . , cn with prefixes Si , the i-th step costs 1 + |∂ Si |, where ∂ X is the set of\nvertices outside X with a neighbour in X. Read backwards as a layout, the cut separating C \\ Si from Si\n== 14\nA coun